# 2026-08-10 (4th dispatch) — THE PATH B FLOOR POPULATION.
#
# WHAT THIS RUN IS FOR, and why it is the full census default rather than a
# targeted pair. The operator's standing ask is a Path B drawdown-tolerance
# floor derived from capital-utilisation + PnL data instead of picked. The
# predictor a floor would sit on is the base book's net_R-per-drawdown RATE —
# and that rate is a property of the LEG, not the cell: every cell swept on a
# leg shares its single value.
#
# So the population that matters is DISTINCT LEGS, not rows. Ten legs x ~10
# cells is ~100 rows over TEN predictor values, and the cells within a leg are
# re-measurements of one book under different levers — they share its trades,
# its regime and its drawdown. `m20_path_b_floor.py` therefore refuses to test
# a floor that does not separate at least 4 distinct legs per side, however
# many cells it has, because a test assuming independence would inflate the
# effective n by the cells-per-leg ratio and hand back a significant-looking p
# for what is really a ten-point comparison. That failure would be worse than
# guessing the floor: it would arrive dressed as significance.
#
# Sweeping two legs would therefore produce `insufficient_population` by
# construction. The census default is the widest leg set already justified by
# measurement (the ten carrying the most R at the giveback ladder's 1R rung),
# so it is the population, not a convenience.
#
# WHAT IS NEW ON THIS RUN, and why the numbers are not comparable to the 3rd
# dispatch's on one axis:
#
#   1. `verdicts.json` now carries `base_book` per window for EVERY leg — the
#      rate above. It was previously computed only inside a Path B candidate's
#      entry, i.e. only for cells that had ALREADY passed the Path B predicate,
#      which conditions the corpus on the outcome (an inefficient book is
#      exactly the one most likely to admit a candidate). This run is the first
#      whose rows carry the axis for legs that produced no candidate at all.
#
#   2. A new `corpus` job merges the run into
#      `docs/research/m20-sweep-corpus.jsonl` and commits it. Before this the
#      evidence went to an Actions artifact no session can download, so every
#      sweep restarted the population from zero.
#
# The cells and the gate are UNCHANGED from the 3rd dispatch — same
# config-exact base (trail-vol threaded), same live-parity geometry
# (tp_cap_pct 0.099), same IS/OOS split. Nothing here re-opens a verdict; it
# records the axis those verdicts were already being judged against.
#
# NOT A PROMOTION RUN. No cell from this sweep ships without the operator's
# Tier-3 call, and the floor itself is a recommendation to be made in the
# morning — with `insufficient_population` a fully acceptable answer if that is
# what the data says.
